# [JMAP](../wg/jmap.html)

## Summary

The JMAP Working Group held a joint session with CALEXT to address ongoing updates across data formats (JSContact, JSCalendar, vCard) and JMAP extensions. Key outcomes included consensus to remove task definitions from JSCalendar bis (moving them to the JMAP tasks specification), agreement to consolidate JSContact updates and errata into an EAI-focused document, and progression of several active JMAP drafts toward Working Group Last Call (WGLC), including `draft-ietf-jmap-mail-sharing` and `draft-ietf-jmap-emailpush`. New extension proposals were also introduced for server-side mail rules, virtual mailboxes, JMAP authorization profiles for AI agents, and a simplified JMAP for Tasks design.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### 1. JSCalendar & JSContact
**Presentation:** [JSCalendar & JSContact](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-jscalendar-jscontact-01) by Robert Stepanek

* **EAI Support:** Robert Stepanek presented the issue where `addr-spec` (RFC 5322) definitions used in JSContact (RFC 9553) and JSCalendar (RFC 8984) disallow internationalized email addresses (RFC 6532). Daniel Gillmor and Lisa Dusseault noted ongoing alignment efforts within JSON Schema (referencing `mailbox` from RFC 5321 / Emailcore). The group discussed updating JSContact email property value spaces, requiring a new document and major version bump.
* **vCard Conversion & Errata:** Address conversion alignment between vCard and JSContact was clarified to place both street name and number in the name field when unseparated. Spanish surname conversion edge cases were fixed. Robert proposed obsoleting RFC 9554 into 9553bis and incorporating errata and updates into a single JSContact update document.
* **JSCalendar Bis & Task Removal:** Robert proposed removing task definitions (VTODO equivalents) from JSCalendar bis to avoid delaying JSCalendar while task modeling undergoes further refinement. Task management updates will be published alongside JMAP for Tasks (`draft-ietf-jmap-tasks`). Two formal polls were conducted (recorded below).
* **iCalendar Extension Draft Consolidation:** Neil Jenkins and Robert Stepanek discussed merging the two active iCalendar/JSCalendar extension drafts into a single document before final publication.

### 2. vCard 4.0 bis
**Presentation:** [vCard 4.0 bis](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-vcard-40-bis-00) by Rick van Rein

* **Errata & Extensions Merging:** Rick van Rein reported that long-standing RFC 6350 errata (escaping, type agreements) have been resolved. Structural parameter encoding (RFC 6868) and place/date of birth/death (RFC 6474) were merged into vCard 4.0 bis. Properties from RFC 9554 remain in the translation specification rather than vCard 4.0 bis.
* **OMA CAB Extensions (RFC 6715):** The Open Mobile Alliance converged address book extensions (hobbies, interests, indexing) were omitted to keep the specification clean, as there was no evidence of active adoption. Ken Murchison supported leaving them as separate extensions. The draft will proceed toward Working Group Last Call.

### 3. Cross-Organization Scheduling / iMIP Replacement
**Presentation:** [JMAP Metadata, Mail Sharing and Refplus extensions + New work ideas](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-metadata-mail-sharing-and-refplus-extensions-new-work-ideas-00) by Mario Loffredo

* **Motivation:** Mario Loffredo highlighted the limitations of iMIP (email-based calendar scheduling) across organization boundaries, noting a lack of delivery status and real-time interaction, forcing reliance on third-party services (e.g., Calendly).
* **Discussion:** Neil Jenkins supported creating a modern, real-time cross-organizational scheduling mechanism based on JSCalendar, free/busy slots, and "book me" availability blocks. He noted that full free/busy exposure is often restricted between companies due to privacy and commercial sensitivity. Neil suggested organizing a dedicated workshop or in-person design meeting to work through the complex protocol and security details.

### 4. JMAP Mail Sharing, Metadata, and Refplus Extensions
**Presentation:** [JMAP Metadata, Mail Sharing and Refplus extensions + New work ideas](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-metadata-mail-sharing-and-refplus-extensions-new-work-ideas-00) by Mario Loffredo

* **`draft-ietf-jmap-mail-sharing`:** Mario noted that Mail Sharing is implemented in Stalwart and Cyrus. Neil Jenkins pointed out a small text clarification regarding implicit owner rights. The document is ready for Working Group Last Call.
* **`draft-ietf-jmap-metadata`:** The metadata draft was updated with copy/import behavior, binary value support, and permissions (`maySetMetadata`). Implementations exist in Stalwart and a Cyrus branch. Mario raised introducing a photography metadata namespace (aperture, location, AI-generated labels) for image files. Neil Jenkins and Bron Gondwana recommended placing the photography metadata registry definition in `draft-ietf-jmap-filenode`.
* **`draft-ietf-jmap-refplus`:** Kept active while awaiting additional server/client implementation experience.

### 5. New Work Proposals: Server-Side Mail Rules and Virtual Mailboxes
**Presentation:** [JMAP Metadata, Mail Sharing and Refplus extensions + New work ideas](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-metadata-mail-sharing-and-refplus-extensions-new-work-ideas-00) by Mario Loffredo

* **Mail Rules Object (`mailRule`):** Mario proposed a standard JMAP object for user mail filtering rules to allow portable UI rule representations across clients without exposing users directly to Sieve complexity. Hans-Jörg Happel, Alexey Melnikov, Neil Jenkins, and Matt Diephouse discussed past attempts and potential challenges with provider rule evaluation models. Alexey Melnikov and Mario Loffredo agreed to coauthor an initial draft to give the working group a concrete proposal to evaluate.
* **Virtual Mailboxes / Saved Searches:** Mario presented an idea to persist JMAP queries as virtual folder objects or metadata so search results run dynamically upon opening. Neil Jenkins noted interest specifically for mail search representations and suggested keeping the design simple. Mario will draft a proposal.

### 6. Email Delivery Push Notifications
**Presentation:** [Email Delivery Push Notifications](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-email-delivery-push-notifications-00) by Neil Jenkins

* **`draft-ietf-jmap-emailpush`:** Neil Jenkins presented recent updates to support pushing badge counts (total or unread message/thread counts for pre-computed mailboxes) over push channels, critical for mobile OS background restrictions. Additionally, requirements were relaxed for IMAP-backed servers using JMAP Core/EmailPush as a stepping stone, requiring only basic mailbox ID filtering. The draft is ready for Working Group Last Call.

### 7. S/MIME Sender Extensions
* **`draft-ietf-jmap-smime-sender-extensions`:** Alexey Melnikov noted that an updated alternative revision of the draft was recently posted to the data tracker and requested working group review.

### 8. File Storage, Blob Ext, Conditional Set, and Object History Extensions
**Presentation:** [filenode et al](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-filenode-et-al-00) by Bron Gondwana

* **`draft-ietf-jmap-filenode`:** Reworked access control, NFC string normalization for filenames, and integration of the photography metadata namespace.
* **`draft-ietf-jmap-blobext`:** Features server-side data transformation (slicing, archiving/zipping, conversion). Alexey Melnikov and Neil Jenkins expressed concerns over spec complexity and asked to narrow the scope to implemented features (e.g., server-side ZIP creation).
* **`draft-ietf-jmap-conditional`:** Added an `atomic` capability and extended `ifUnchanged` checks to un-updated objects. WGLC will wait for a second implementation (Mario Loffredo).
* **`draft-ietf-jmap-object-history`:** Added a `restore` property on `set` to allow restoring previously destroyed objects.

### 9. JMAP for AI
**Presentation:** [JMAP for AI](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-jmap-for-ai-00) by Arnt Gulbrandsen

* **Concept:** Arnt Gulbrandsen proposed documenting JMAP operational guidance and restricted authorization profiles so that AI agents/LLMs default to least-privilege access (e.g., stripping send or write privileges).
* **Discussion:** Daniel Gillmor, Neil Jenkins, and Jim Fenton discussed whether this requires JMAP protocol changes or finer-grained OAuth authorization scopes implemented by providers. Discussion will continue on the mailing list.

### 10. JMAP for Task Lists Proposal
**Presentation:** [JMAP for Task Lists proposal](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-jmap-jmap-for-task-lists-proposal-00) by Rick van Rein

* **`draft-ietf-jmap-tasks`:** Rick van Rein proposed a simplified specification for task list management aligned with recent JSCalendar/JSContact updates, reducing optional core iCalendar features. Hans-Jörg Happel (coauthor of `draft-ietf-jmap-tasks`) agreed to collaborate with Rick to update the existing working group draft text wholesale.

